<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baden-Württemberg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baden-Württemberg is a state in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>. It was created in 1952 by merging Württemberg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, Württemberg-Hohenzollern and South Baden, uniting the historical lands of Baden, Württemberg and Hohenzollern. It is one of Germany's most prosperous states and a centre of the car and engineering industries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="Baden-Württemberg" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Verfassungsausschuss des Landes Baden-Württemberg (Landesregierung) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Baden-W%C3%BCrttemberg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
